F4 STATUS
CLOSED — PASS — 11/11 verdicts true on each of Bun 1.4.0, Bun 1.4.2 and Node 24.21.0: both live replacement shapes delivered the post-replacement write with no poll batch, the injected close path behaved (old close ignored, dead watch re-armed and delivered, close cancelled the timers), and the idle directory stayed silent.

THE SENTENCE THE EVIDENCE SUPPORTS
The watcher recovers WITHOUT a poll when a watched directory is deleted or renamed: it accepts the directory's own self-event (the signal Bun 1.4.0/1.4.2 and Node 24 emit here — measured), decides identity by inode AND birthtime (an rm -rf + mkdir reuses the inode, measured), and — on a runtime that reports a handle close/error, or when a test injects one — also re-arms on a bounded 25/100/400 ms schedule. A file written after either replacement is delivered, and an untouched directory stays silent. After the three bounded attempts the watch gives up SILENTLY, so a caller that needs a guarantee beyond that must keep a resync path.

SEAM JUDGMENT
ACCEPTABLE as an optional, documented test seam. — No product caller: `grep -rn createWatch packages/*/src` finds it only in mpd-graph-core, and the default is `fs.watch`.; The interface's own doc says 'present only for a test that drives close itself', and the README's user-facing options line omits it (it lists debounceMs/onChange/onError).; The codebase already carries the same pattern at a higher level: `GraphModelDeps.watch` exists so an arm can drive batches without a filesystem.; It is what makes the close path provable at all on these runtimes, which is strictly better than an untested subscription. Residual: If maximal hygiene is wanted, the seam can stay library-internal by exporting a test-only constructor; that is a polish call, not a defect.

